That confirms it — the browser on your Linux machine is too old to support `crypto.randomUUID()` (requires Chrome 92+, Firefox 95+, Safari 15.4+).

A simple fix is to add a polyfill. Want me to replace `crypto.randomUUID()` calls with a fallback like this?

```typescript
function generateUUID(): string {
  if (typeof crypto !== 'undefined' && crypto.randomUUID) {
    return crypto.randomUUID();
  }
  // Fallback for older browsers
  return '10000000-1000-4000-8000-100000000000'.replace(/[018]/g, c =>
    (+c ^ crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(1))[0] & 15 >> +c / 4).toString(16)
  );
}
```

This uses `crypto.randomUUID()` when available, and falls back to `crypto.getRandomValues()` (which has much broader support) otherwise.

Should I apply this fix?

